I think the answer depends on whether the repository becomes a living practice or just a library.

If it remains a library, you’ll have an interesting collection of frameworks, metaphors, and engineering patterns.

If it becomes a practice—where ideas are tested, refined, reused, and connected to real projects—it compounds much more like a software ecosystem or scientific notebook than like a book. That’s because each new artifact can improve how later artifacts are created.

I actually like your use of momentum as the metaphor, but I’d extend it.

In physics, momentum isn’t just speed. It’s mass and velocity.

The Pantheon analogue might be:

\text{Semantic Momentum}
=
\text{Corpus Quality}
\times
\text{Rate of Iteration}

Where:

* Corpus Quality is the depth, clarity, testability, and reusability of the repository.
* Rate of Iteration is how often those ideas are exercised, improved, and applied to real problems.

A large corpus that never gets used has little practical momentum.

A small but actively refined corpus can have a surprisingly large effect.

What I think the repo can realistically yield over time is:

* A common vocabulary that lets collaborators communicate more efficiently.
* Reusable design patterns for prompting, reasoning, documentation, and engineering.
* Faster onboarding, because newcomers learn operators instead of isolated tricks.
* Cross-domain transfer, where an idea developed in one context inspires another.
* A historical record of what worked, what failed, and how the ideas evolved.

Those are all forms of compounding that don’t require assuming every idea is correct—they require continuously testing and improving them.

One line I would add to the repo is:

Momentum is stored work. Compounding is momentum that survives translation.

That ties together several of your recurring themes:

* compression without losing reconstructability,
* representation independence,
* provenance and revision,
* and turning isolated insights into reusable operators.

If the Pantheon consistently captures ideas in a form that other people—or future versions of yourselves—can reconstruct, critique, and extend, then each artifact becomes less like a note and more like a stepping stone. That’s the kind of momentum that can persist beyond any single conversation.